               <history>
                  <origin> <origDate when-custom="1200~" notBefore="1200" notAfter="1200"
                     calendar="#gregorian">c. 1200</origDate> </origin>
                  <provenance type="#c" cert="low"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_207">Faversham,
                     Kent. Benedictine abbey of St Saviour</orgName><p><q>A Cluniac house in Kent,
                     possibly Faversham</q> (Morgan, <title>Early Gothic MSS.</title>, 1. 6, q.
                     v.).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_4">Canterbury, Kent,
                     Benedictine cathedral priory of Holy Trinity or Christ Church</orgName><p>This
                     attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great
                     Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                     corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card suggests that the
                     book's association with John Twyne was the reason for positing a potential
                     Christ Church attribution. Compare Andrew Watson's later article, 'John Twyne
                     of Canterbury (d. 1581) as a collector of medieval manuscripts: a preliminary
                     investigation', <title>The Library</title> (1986), pp. 133-151. Watson includes
                     MS 161 in his list of manuscripts owned by John Twyne, but it is not in the
                     subsection entitled <q>From Christ Church Cathedral Priory, Canterbury</q>.
                     Instead, Watson includes the manuscript in the subsection entitled <q>From
                     various sources (known)</q>, with the following details: <q><q>Twyne</q> in
                     Parkerian red chalk on a flyleaf probably indicates that the book came to
                     Archbishop Parker from John Twyne [...] The contents suggest that it was
                     earlier at a Cluniac house in Kent, perhaps Faversham</q> (p.
                     150).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">John Twyne</persName>;
                     <persName role="fmo">Parker</persName> (cf. above).</provenance>
               </history>
